WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.057
The order belongs under Lab. This backup
puts it under Studio. Restore Drill

00:00:05.057 --> 00:00:07.270
blocked it before writing anything.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:13.128
Here is a client's field agreement. The
team finds it by opening Studio, then

00:00:13.128 --> 00:00:13.694
Licences.

00:00:13.844 --> 00:00:18.586
After restoring a backup, the title,
address and tags can all survive while the

00:00:18.586 --> 00:00:20.627
route to the agreement disappears.

00:00:20.777 --> 00:00:25.091
Restore Drill tests that route in a
browser before anyone relies on the

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:25.516
backup.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:31.326
For this run we used an editable workspace
and the published Linkwarden version two

00:00:31.326 --> 00:00:33.638
point seven point one export controller.

00:00:33.788 --> 00:00:38.719
The controller ran against the workspace
records. A separate database received the

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:39.621
exported bytes.

00:00:39.771 --> 00:00:45.228
The restored copy still has three folders
and one bookmark. Look at Licences. Its

00:00:45.228 --> 00:00:46.710
parent is now unknown.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.936
Kane CLI gets one plain language task.
Starting at the root, open the client

00:00:52.936 --> 00:00:58.347
folder, open Licences, then verify the
agreement's full path, address, tags and

00:00:58.347 --> 00:00:59.717
parent relationship.

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:04.321
The saved browser run passes on the
source. On the restored copy, Kane

00:01:04.321 --> 00:01:08.521
identifies the missing parent and returns
a confirmed product bug.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:14.274
Could a smarter importer recover the
owner? Open the source workspace and run

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:15.445
its ambiguity test.

00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:20.665
The tool builds two valid sets of
collection records. In one, the agreement

00:01:20.665 --> 00:01:24.113
belongs to Studio. In the other, it
belongs to Lab.

00:01:24.263 --> 00:01:28.730
The historical exporter produces exactly
the same five hundred and thirty nine

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.969
bytes for both. The portable exporter
distinguishes them and restores both

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:33.312
paths.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:39.486
Kane's finding and its frozen browser task
went to Codex. Here is the candidate from

00:01:39.486 --> 00:01:40.837
the reviewed response.

00:01:40.987 --> 00:01:45.892
The highlighted line builds its map from
every collection, including folders with

00:01:45.892 --> 00:01:47.224
zero direct bookmarks.

00:01:47.374 --> 00:01:51.955
The next lines resolve the child's parent
from that same map. A child can even

00:01:51.955 --> 00:01:54.891
appear before its parent in the database
response.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.658
We ran that candidate, imported its bytes
into a fresh database, and asked Kane to

00:02:00.658 --> 00:02:02.446
repeat the frozen browser task.

00:02:02.596 --> 00:02:07.450
On a fresh restore, Kane can now open the
agreement under Studio. On a second

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.728
workspace, it retrieves a different order
under Lab.

00:02:10.878 --> 00:02:14.027
The independent archive comparisons also
match.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.368
What if the exporter simply picks the
first root when it cannot identify the

00:02:19.368 --> 00:02:22.834
parent? That patch happens to recover the
original agreement.

00:02:22.984 --> 00:02:28.282
Here are seven actual archive comparisons,
including reordered folders, empty

00:02:28.282 --> 00:02:31.172
ancestors, and identically named branches.

00:02:31.322 --> 00:02:36.303
The reviewed repair passes all seven. The
shortcut matches three and puts the second

00:02:36.303 --> 00:02:39.268
workspace's purchase order under the wrong
client.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:45.531
We also handed that wrong repair to Kane.
Both browser checks pass on the original

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:46.170
workspace.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.853
On the independent source, Kane opens Lab,
then Purchases, then the order. On the

00:02:51.853 --> 00:02:56.020
independent restore, it opens Lab and
finds the folder empty.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:01.012
Kane returns a confirmed product bug. The
first green result would have certified

00:03:01.012 --> 00:03:01.968
the wrong owner.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:07.591
We also ran two installations of
Linkwarden itself. The original contract

00:03:07.591 --> 00:03:09.616
is under South, then Agreements.

00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:14.839
After the stock migration, Agreements
appears at the root. Both tags and all

00:03:14.839 --> 00:03:16.308
three folders survive.

00:03:16.458 --> 00:03:20.860
We captured the known source and wrote its
folder relationships into a separate empty

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:25.263
account through Linkwarden's own APIs. The
recovered contract is back under South and

00:03:25.263 --> 00:03:25.832
Agreements.

00:03:25.982 --> 00:03:30.774
Kane checked the same native collections
grid in all three accounts. Source passed,

00:03:30.774 --> 00:03:33.314
stock migration failed, and recovery
passed.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:38.158
We ran the same reviewed candidate
directly against a real Linkwarden

00:03:38.158 --> 00:03:40.544
database using its generated Prisma
client.

00:03:40.694 --> 00:03:45.703
The candidate archive preserves the path.
But the stock importer still flattens it.

00:03:45.853 --> 00:03:50.015
Restoring those same candidate bytes
through the mapped writer preserves the

00:03:50.015 --> 00:03:53.300
path. Export and import both have to carry
the relationship.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.994
In another production run, two clients had
identically titled contracts, and six

00:03:58.994 --> 00:04:00.219
folders between them.

00:04:00.369 --> 00:04:05.481
After stock migration, only Agreements or
Renewals remained in the paths. The mapped

00:04:05.481 --> 00:04:10.290
restore recovered both complete paths,
including the extra parent level for the

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:10.777
renewal.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:16.974
A separate unattended run resumed Kane's
saved failure. A local coding model called

00:04:16.974 --> 00:04:21.765
Qwen generated the condition that keeps
collection identifiers in the export.

00:04:21.915 --> 00:04:26.294
Kane verified the repair on the original
agreement and the independent purchase

00:04:26.294 --> 00:04:28.013
order, each in a fresh restore.

00:04:28.163 --> 00:04:30.731
The original archive still needs review.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.713
Here is the holdout library after stock
Linkwarden imports it. December appears at

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:37.557
the top level.

00:04:37.707 --> 00:04:42.621
With the importer patch, both libraries
preserved every path. Kane passed both

00:04:42.621 --> 00:04:43.691
checks in Chrome.

00:04:43.841 --> 00:04:48.324
The blind attempts produced no usable
patch. The repository includes their

00:04:48.324 --> 00:04:50.383
records and the tested Codex diff.
